  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 326 AM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\n(Saturday Night - Sunday)\n\nSurface high pressure continues to move southward through the region \nSaturday night as an upper level trough extends southwest of a \nclosed upper level low over New England. The trough has relatively \nlittle impact other than reinforcing the northwesterly flow aloft, \nwhile surface winds remain light under mostly clear skies. Another \nnight of near-freezing lows is expected with the coolest \ntemperatures in the upper 20s to low-30s affecting locations along \nand south of I-70. The Freeze Warning issued for late Friday and \nearly Saturday morning will effectively put an end to frost/freeze \nheadlines for the remainder of the season, but those taking \nadvantage of the recent late-season warmth should remain cognizant \nof near to sub-freezing lows early Sunday morning. Westerly surface \nflow around the northern periphery of the ridge moderates \ntemperatures back to near normal Sunday with highs in the low to mid-\n60s. \n\n(Sunday Night - Next Friday)\n\nThe general concepts of a fall El Nino pattern seem evident over the \ncourse of the week. Cold air remains well north of the region with \nany sensible rain potential well to the south through at least \nWednesday. Another surface high builds eastward from the Plains \nunder quasi-zonal flow aloft. Mild mid-level air is carried eastward \nfrom the Plains into the mid-Mississippi Valley through Wednesday. \nMonday/Tuesday are favored to be the warmest with 850mb temperatures \nin the low to mid-teens. A weak mid-level trough quickly moves west \nto east late Tuesday into Wednesday with slight cooling in the the \nmid-level profile, bring temperatures closer to normal (low-60s) \nnorth of I-70 and mid to upper 60s to the south. \n\nThe late week pattern remains less certain with LREF 500MB spread \nrapidly increase from late Wednesday into Thursday. The spread stems \nfrom the strength of a longwave trough and how much of the eastern \nCONUS is enveloped by the longwave feature. This ends up being the \ndifference between a brief, reinforcement in cold air (GFS) or \na better sustained push (ECMWF) Thursday into Friday. NBM \nspreads produce low chances (15-20%) for showers as a weak \nsurface low tracks west to east ahead of the cold front late \nWednesday into Thursday. However, with uncertainty in the upper \nlevel pattern, there is low confidence in upper support due to \ndifferences in timing, strength, and location of an upper level \nshortwave rotating southward down the western side of the \nlongwave trough. Moisture also looks to be at a premium with \nlittle moisture return, suggesting that if anything \nmaterializes, it will largely be light and will pass through \nrather quickly with dry conditions largely favored at this time.",
